From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 00:04:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: ignore userspace keepalives once the system goes
 down

With stop_on_reboot=0 a running watchdog is kept running through a
reboot, so that a reboot that hangs (for example in device_shutdown()
on a device lock left held by an earlier oops) still ends in a reset.

A forced reboot (systemctl reboot -ff) calls reboot(2) directly while
the service manager keeps running and keeps pinging its watchdog, so
such a hang is never caught. Once system_state says the system is
halting, powering off or restarting, ignore keepalives.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/watchdog/watchdog_dev.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/watchdog/watchdog_dev.c b/drivers/watchdog/watchdog_dev.c
index d789500..34cc3c3 100644
--- a/drivers/watchdog/watchdog_dev.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/watchdog_dev.c
@@ -195,6 +195,16 @@ static int watchdog_ping(struct watchdog_device *wdd)
 	if (!watchdog_hw_running(wdd))
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * A watchdog kept running through a reboot (stop_on_reboot=0) must
+	 * catch a reboot that hangs. Userspace still runs then (a forced
+	 * reboot leaves the service manager running), so its keepalives no
+	 * longer count.
+	 */
+	if (system_state == SYSTEM_HALT || system_state == SYSTEM_POWER_OFF ||
+	    system_state == SYSTEM_RESTART)
+		return 0;
+
 	set_bit(_WDOG_KEEPALIVE, &wd_data->status);
 
 	wd_data->last_keepalive = ktime_get();
-- 
